<article class="env-lemma" id="0BC1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BC1">Lemma <span data-tag="0BC1">10.120.10</span>.</a> A polynomial ring over a UFD is a UFD. In particular, if $k$ is a field, then $k[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$ is a UFD. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $R$ be a UFD. Then $R$ satisfies the ascending chain condition for principal ideals (Lemma <a href="/tag/0BUD" data-tag="0BUD">10.120.8</a>), hence $R[x]$ satisfies the ascending chain condition for principal ideals (Lemma <a href="/tag/0BUE" data-tag="0BUE">10.120.9</a>), and hence every element of $R[x]$ has a factorization into irreducibles (Lemma <a href="/tag/034R" data-tag="034R">10.120.3</a>). Let $S \subset R$ be the multiplicative subset generated by prime elements. Since every nonunit of $R$ is a product of prime elements we see that $K = S^{-1}R$ is the fraction field of $R$. Observe that every prime element of $R$ maps to a prime element of $R[x]$ and that $S^{-1}(R[x]) = S^{-1}R[x] = K[x]$ is a UFD (and even a PID). Thus we may apply Lemma <a href="/tag/0AFU" data-tag="0AFU">10.120.7</a> to conclude. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>